      "content": "Vietnam is generally seen as more reliable for long-term contracts due to a stronger emphasis on formal business etiquette and written agreements, whereas Nigerian suppliers often prioritize personal relationships and flexibility, which can lead to inconsistent adherence to strict deadlines. Communication styles in Vietnam tend to be more hierarchical and indirect to maintain harmony, while Nigerian business culture is often described as more direct, informal, and relationship-driven, requiring a different approach to negotiation and conflict resolution. A logistics manager should expect higher initial setup friction in Nigeria due to infrastructure challenges and bureaucratic variability, whereas Vietnam offers a more predictable, albeit slower, bureaucratic process that aligns better with standardized international supply chain protocols. Ultimately, while Vietnam presents a lower risk profile for operational consistency, Nigeria offers greater potential for rapid scaling if the team is willing to navigate a more volatile and less formalized environment.",
